From c8e8078c8bb700cdac2c42136d982e8d27308afe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 00:42:12 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] theme: add the Zeo theme family and make it the default

Zeo's accent was literally Zed's `info` colour -- text.accent and info are the
same string in both One variants -- so the brand had no independent hue at all.
It is now Amethyst: #BD93F9 dark, #7C3AED light. Two hexes, because one cannot
serve both modes at AA. In the light variant `info` is desaturated and rotated
to 204 degrees, taking its separation from the accent from 35 to 59 degrees, so
both variants share one hue architecture.

The elevated surface had no background step whatsoever: elevated_surface and
surface are byte-identical in One, so an "elevated" surface was separated from
the one beneath it by nothing but a black drop shadow, which a dark canvas
swallows. That is the mechanical cause of the flat look. Zeo ships a real
four-rung ladder -- canvas, surface, elevated, modal -- each rung a step the eye
can actually see, with modal_surface.background set so the top rung exists at
all rather than falling back onto the one below it.

Syntax is derived from One Dark and harmonised with the accent, not authored from
scratch: code stays legible and familiar from minute one. One Light's indigo is
retired -- it collides with the Amethyst and already failed AA at 3.84:1
upstream.

The theme is keyed by the `name` field inside the JSON, not by the filename. A
mismatch with default.json does not fail the build, does not fail the app, and
shows no error -- it silently falls back to One Dark, which would ship a Zeo
build that looks exactly like Zed. The two strings are pinned against each other
by a test. The file must also stay strict JSON: the bundled loader does not
accept the comments the user-theme parser tolerates, and would fail the same
silent way.

Registration needs no code: themes are embedded by directory glob and discovered
by listing. Every Zed theme remains selectable.

Covers R2.1, R3.1-R3.8.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
